# tests/T05.bd - T05: call surcharge and the observer effect.
#
# ONE QUESTION: does making a CALL cost anything on top of the one tick the
# source line it sits on already costs - and above all, does get_cpu()?
#
# WHY THIS ONE MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS
#   Four official files price get_cpu(), get_unix_time() and the builtins at
#   "Cost: 0 ticks". field_notes.txt lists get_cpu() under "Confirmed
#   genuinely 0 ticks (these were right)" at line ~74 - and then at line ~442
#   carries a CORRECTION saying the player observed get_cpu() adding load
#   after all, with no magnitude ever attached to it. Both statements still
#   stand in the same file today. That unquantified correction is now
#   load-bearing: it is the stated reason the extra-throttle experiment
#   (+3.8s per item, crash moved ~70s EARLIER) was read as self-defeating, the
#   stated explanation for the pinned cpu=100 / cpu=80 readings, and the
#   justification for the entire "fewer checks, not more" strategy pivot.
#   Nothing has ever A/B'd it. This script does, and it is the cheapest
#   experiment in the whole programme.
#
# METHOD - seven arms, one run, wall clock ONLY
#   get_cpu() is the SUBJECT here, so it is never used as the instrument: the
#   only observable is elapsed real time. Every arm is a byte-for-byte
#   identical function - same line count, same loop, same sleeps, same
#   bracket - differing ONLY in the right-hand side of six measured lines:
#     CTRL  b = 1                                        baseline: bare line
#     GCPU  b = get_cpu()                                the suspect
#     TIME  b = get_unix_time()                          the ruler itself
#     ISTR  b = str(7)                                   a builtin
#     UFNC  b = f0()                                     a user call frame
#     ADDS  b = r + r + r + r        4 leaves, 3 adds, NO calls (DENS control)
#     DENS  b = get_cpu()+get_cpu()+get_cpu()+get_cpu()  4 CALLS on ONE line
#   ADDS/DENS are a matched pair: same expression tree shape, four leaves
#   each, the leaves differing only by being a variable read vs a call. They
#   are what separates "charged per call" from "charged per line".
#
# READING IT - every delta is against the SAME run's CTRL, never another run
#   d_X       = mean burst of arm X  -  mean burst of CTRL   (seconds)
#   per-call  = d_X / 6                       (six measured lines, 1 call each)
#   in ticks  = per-call / seconds-per-tick from T01. At base tier (10
#               ticks/sec, ~0.1 s/tick) a d_X of +0.6s == exactly 1 extra
#               tick per call.
#   VERDICTS:
#     every |d| < 0.15s  -> calls are free beyond their own line. The docs are
#         right, the field_notes CORRECTION is wrong, and every conclusion
#         resting on "get_cpu() adds load" reopens.
#     d_gcpu >= 0.4s in BOTH runs -> the surcharge is REAL. throttle()'s
#         `while get_cpu() > t: sleep(1)` is provably feeding the meter it is
#         waiting on, and every get_cpu()-guarded number in field_notes needs
#         a correction factor.
#     d_ufnc >= 1.0s -> a real call frame, roughly 2 ticks (frame + the
#         callee's own `return` line).
#     (dens-adds)/24 ~= d_gcpu/6 -> charging is PER CALL.
#     dens ~= adds              -> charging is PER LINE; extra calls on the
#                                  same line ride along free.
#     d_adds >= 0.4s           -> operators/variable reads are charged too,
#                                  i.e. per expression NODE, not per line.
#   INVALID RUN: if CTRL is not the smallest mean, report it and re-run -
#   something drifted and no delta from that run can be trusted.
#
# THE BURST IS THE EXPERIMENT (deliberate, not an oversight)
#   The six measured lines are unpaced on purpose - that is what is being
#   priced - and are bracketed by sleep(1) on both sides so every arm starts
#   from the same decayed meter state. Worst case is DENS: if every call costs
#   1 tick that is 6 x (1 line + 4 calls) = 30 ticks = ~3.0s of continuous
#   load at base tier, inside the 5s kill window. GCPU only dies if a call
#   costs ~6+ ticks. IF AN ARM IS KILLED, THAT IS A RESULT, NOT A FAILURE:
#   DENS dying while GCPU survives means a call costs >= ~1.5 ticks AND
#   charging is per call. GCPU dying at all means the surcharge is enormous.
#
# ARM ORDER is risk-ordered on purpose: CTRL and GCPU run first so the
#   headline A/B is banked within the first ~45s, because the single most
#   reliably observed hazard in this whole investigation is a run dying at an
#   unpredictable time (5 identical runs died at +40.7/+111.0/+40.3/+65.5/
#   +64.3s). DENS - the only arm whose design makes a kill likely - runs last,
#   after its own ADDS control, so its death costs nothing else.
#
# HOW TO RUN
#   `T05`  - no parameters, one execution, ~2.5 minutes.
#   Run it FIRST THING in a fresh session with nothing else running (confirm
#   via `ps` / `jobs`). Paste back EVERY R..., ARM-..., SUM... line.
#   REPEATS: 2 runs, same session, back to back. A third only if d_gcpu lands
#   on opposite sides of the 0.15s / 0.40s decision band between the two.
#
# IF IT DIES WITH ZERO OUTPUT: this file is ~250 lines and a top-level
#   script's own func declarations cost ticks. The sleep(1) lines between the
#   arm definitions below exist to absorb that. If it still dies before
#   printing anything, halve the file - delete the ISTR, UFNC, ADDS and DENS
#   arms and their driver blocks, keep CTRL and GCPU - and re-run.

func f0():
    return 1

sleep(1)

func arm_ctrl(t0):
    var s = 0
    var r = 0
    var b = 0
    var ts = 0
    var el = 0
    while r < 5:
        sleep(1)
        ts = get_unix_time()
        b = 1
        b = 1
        b = 1
        b = 1
        b = 1
        b = 1
        el = get_unix_time() - ts
        sleep(1)
        s = s + el
        r = r + 1
        log("R" + str(r) + " arm=CTRL burst=" + str(el) + " at=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))
    return s / 5

sleep(1)

func arm_gcpu(t0):
    var s = 0
    var r = 0
    var b = 0
    var ts = 0
    var el = 0
    while r < 5:
        sleep(1)
        ts = get_unix_time()
        b = get_cpu()
        b = get_cpu()
        b = get_cpu()
        b = get_cpu()
        b = get_cpu()
        b = get_cpu()
        el = get_unix_time() - ts
        sleep(1)
        s = s + el
        r = r + 1
        log("R" + str(r) + " arm=GCPU burst=" + str(el) + " at=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))
    return s / 5

sleep(1)

func arm_time(t0):
    var s = 0
    var r = 0
    var b = 0
    var ts = 0
    var el = 0
    while r < 5:
        sleep(1)
        ts = get_unix_time()
        b = get_unix_time()
        b = get_unix_time()
        b = get_unix_time()
        b = get_unix_time()
        b = get_unix_time()
        b = get_unix_time()
        el = get_unix_time() - ts
        sleep(1)
        s = s + el
        r = r + 1
        log("R" + str(r) + " arm=TIME burst=" + str(el) + " at=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))
    return s / 5

sleep(1)

func arm_istr(t0):
    var s = 0
    var r = 0
    var b = 0
    var ts = 0
    var el = 0
    while r < 5:
        sleep(1)
        ts = get_unix_time()
        b = str(7)
        b = str(7)
        b = str(7)
        b = str(7)
        b = str(7)
        b = str(7)
        el = get_unix_time() - ts
        sleep(1)
        s = s + el
        r = r + 1
        log("R" + str(r) + " arm=ISTR burst=" + str(el) + " at=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))
    return s / 5

sleep(1)

func arm_ufnc(t0):
    var s = 0
    var r = 0
    var b = 0
    var ts = 0
    var el = 0
    while r < 5:
        sleep(1)
        ts = get_unix_time()
        b = f0()
        b = f0()
        b = f0()
        b = f0()
        b = f0()
        b = f0()
        el = get_unix_time() - ts
        sleep(1)
        s = s + el
        r = r + 1
        log("R" + str(r) + " arm=UFNC burst=" + str(el) + " at=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))
    return s / 5

sleep(1)

func arm_adds(t0):
    var s = 0
    var r = 0
    var b = 0
    var ts = 0
    var el = 0
    while r < 5:
        sleep(1)
        ts = get_unix_time()
        b = r + r + r + r
        b = r + r + r + r
        b = r + r + r + r
        b = r + r + r + r
        b = r + r + r + r
        b = r + r + r + r
        el = get_unix_time() - ts
        sleep(1)
        s = s + el
        r = r + 1
        log("R" + str(r) + " arm=ADDS burst=" + str(el) + " at=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))
    return s / 5

sleep(1)

func arm_dens(t0):
    var s = 0
    var r = 0
    var b = 0
    var ts = 0
    var el = 0
    while r < 5:
        sleep(1)
        ts = get_unix_time()
        b = get_cpu() + get_cpu() + get_cpu() + get_cpu()
        b = get_cpu() + get_cpu() + get_cpu() + get_cpu()
        b = get_cpu() + get_cpu() + get_cpu() + get_cpu()
        b = get_cpu() + get_cpu() + get_cpu() + get_cpu()
        b = get_cpu() + get_cpu() + get_cpu() + get_cpu()
        b = get_cpu() + get_cpu() + get_cpu() + get_cpu()
        el = get_unix_time() - ts
        sleep(1)
        s = s + el
        r = r + 1
        log("R" + str(r) + " arm=DENS burst=" + str(el) + " at=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))
    return s / 5

sleep(1)

# --- driver: seven arms in risk order, each bracketed by its own boundary
# --- lines. The one get_cpu() in each boundary log is outside every timed
# --- bracket and appears exactly twice per arm, identically, so it cannot
# --- leak into any delta - it is there to prove every arm STARTED from the
# --- same meter state, which neither source draft checked.

var t0 = get_unix_time()
sleep(2)
log("CALLPRICE start arms=7 rounds=5 lines=6 cpu=" + str(get_cpu()) + " at=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))

sleep(2)
log("ARM-START arm=CTRL cpu=" + str(get_cpu()) + " at=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))
var mCTRL = arm_ctrl(t0)
log("ARM-DONE arm=CTRL mean=" + str(mCTRL) + " cpu=" + str(get_cpu()) + " at=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))

sleep(2)
log("ARM-START arm=GCPU cpu=" + str(get_cpu()) + " at=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))
var mGCPU = arm_gcpu(t0)
log("ARM-DONE arm=GCPU mean=" + str(mGCPU) + " cpu=" + str(get_cpu()) + " at=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))

sleep(2)
log("ARM-START arm=TIME cpu=" + str(get_cpu()) + " at=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))
var mTIME = arm_time(t0)
log("ARM-DONE arm=TIME mean=" + str(mTIME) + " cpu=" + str(get_cpu()) + " at=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))

sleep(2)
log("ARM-START arm=ISTR cpu=" + str(get_cpu()) + " at=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))
var mISTR = arm_istr(t0)
log("ARM-DONE arm=ISTR mean=" + str(mISTR) + " cpu=" + str(get_cpu()) + " at=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))

sleep(2)
log("ARM-START arm=UFNC cpu=" + str(get_cpu()) + " at=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))
var mUFNC = arm_ufnc(t0)
log("ARM-DONE arm=UFNC mean=" + str(mUFNC) + " cpu=" + str(get_cpu()) + " at=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))

sleep(2)
log("ARM-START arm=ADDS cpu=" + str(get_cpu()) + " at=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))
var mADDS = arm_adds(t0)
log("ARM-DONE arm=ADDS mean=" + str(mADDS) + " cpu=" + str(get_cpu()) + " at=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))

sleep(2)
log("ARM-START arm=DENS cpu=" + str(get_cpu()) + " at=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))
var mDENS = arm_dens(t0)
log("ARM-DONE arm=DENS mean=" + str(mDENS) + " cpu=" + str(get_cpu()) + " at=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))

sleep(2)
log("SUM1 ctrl=" + str(mCTRL) + " gcpu=" + str(mGCPU) + " d_gcpu=" + str(mGCPU - mCTRL))
sleep(1)
log("SUM2 time=" + str(mTIME) + " d_time=" + str(mTIME - mCTRL) + " istr=" + str(mISTR) + " d_istr=" + str(mISTR - mCTRL))
sleep(1)
log("SUM3 ufnc=" + str(mUFNC) + " d_ufnc=" + str(mUFNC - mCTRL) + " adds=" + str(mADDS) + " d_adds=" + str(mADDS - mCTRL))
sleep(1)
log("SUM4 dens=" + str(mDENS) + " dens_minus_adds=" + str(mDENS - mADDS))
sleep(1)
log("SUM5 per_call_sparse=" + str((mGCPU - mCTRL) / 6) + "s per_call_dense=" + str((mDENS - mADDS) / 24) + "s (equal => per-call; dense~0 => per-line)")
sleep(1)
log("CALLPRICE DONE 7/7 arms x 5 rounds, total=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + "s - paste EVERY R/ARM/SUM line")
